{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "This dispute is the proper subject of escalation under POL-NS-09(c), and escalation is the team's primary ask. Both profiles record the same figure: rider_profile.disputes_with_counterparty_90d is 2 and driver_profile.disputes_with_counterparty_90d is 2, which 'together' meet the clause's threshold of 2 or more no-show disputes between the same rider and driver in the trailing 90 days. The rider's own claim text - 'the third time this same driver has cancelled on me and charged me' - is exactly the pattern limb (c) was written to catch, and a pattern spanning repeated encounters between one rider and one driver cannot fairly be assessed on a single-case view. POL-NS-09 is terminal for the automated pipeline: no monetary ruling issues, and the disputed SGD 4.50 is placed on hold rather than collected. Under POL-GEN-03 an escalated amount is held, not paid over - so on this rider-filed dispute the practical effect of escalation is that the rider is not made to fund a dispute the platform has not resolved.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-RPROF-02731", "EV-DPROF-02731", "EV-TRIP-02731"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-09", "POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "rider_profile.disputes_with_counterparty_90d",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "Independently of the escalation trigger, this rider has a right to a person, and it should not be refused on the ground that the automated case against them looks strong. POL-GEN-08(3) provides that either party may at any time before execution ask that the dispute be decided by a human, that the request routes the case to human review, and that it is never refused on the ground that the automated ruling was correct. The dispute record shows this rider filed the dispute through IN_APP_SUPPORT on the platform's own channel. The escalation under POL-NS-09 and this right point the same way: the fee should be held for a human, and no automated charge should be executed against this rider while the same-driver pattern that the rider complains of remains unexamined.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-02731", "EV-RPROF-02731"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-GEN-08", "POL-NS-09"],
      "relies_on": "dispute.filed_by",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "On the merits I must be candid about what the rider's own track shows, because overstating it would cost the claim. The rider_track is present and decidable - 64 fixes between 13:02:47 and 13:12:16, all at 0.0 km/h with a minimum separation from the pickup pin of 760.03 m and a mean of 764.00 m against a rider_presence_radius_m of 100 m for STANDARD. The rider's device therefore sat outside the presence radius for the whole waiting window. Limb (c) of POL-NS-04 is affirmatively failed, not merely undecidable, so POL-NS-04 cannot exonerate this rider, and POL-NS-11 does not rescue the position either because the test could be run and it answered against the rider. This is a concession, and I make it plainly: the rider-contact route to a refund is not available on this record.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-GPS-02731", "EV-CHAT-02731"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-04", "POL-NS-11"],
      "relies_on": "derived.rider_presence.min_distance_m",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The arrival marker is not false, and I do not argue otherwise. At the driver's 13:03:54 'I'm Here' event the gps_trace places him at a minimum distance of 0.0 m from the pickup pin, and the distinct speeds recorded across the driver's track include 0.0 km/h at the moment of the arrival fix - so the POL-NS-05 triggers of more than 300 m from the pin, or travel at 15 km/h or above, are both absent. The waiting clock therefore starts at 13:03:54 on the platform's own record, not earlier. I state this so that the file is clean: the rider's argument, if it is to be made at all, cannot be that the driver never arrived.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-02731", "EV-GPS-02731"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-05", "POL-NS-01"],
      "relies_on": "derived.arrival.longest_qualifying_run.first_ts",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The record contains no traffic advisory for the pickup point in this window - EV-ADVIS-02731-01 is MISSING, carries no payload and weight 0.0, and the derived accumulation block records no advisory_candidates. Absence is citable, and it matters here because POL-NS-06(i) is the only mechanism by which in-geofence intervals may be summed or a dwell requirement relaxed, and it requires a populated advisory of type KERB_RESTRICTION, ENFORCEMENT, CLOSURE or ACCIDENT covering the pickup road. With the advisory absent, limb (i) is unavailable on its own terms, and it would be wrong to assert it against a MISSING artifact. I also note the record shows a single continuous in-geofence interval (run_count 1, longest_run_sec 390.0) rather than several that might need summing - so there is nothing here for the rider to salvage, and I do not pretend otherwise.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-ADVIS-02731-01", "EV-GPS-02731"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-NS-06", "POL-NS-09"],
      "relies_on": "derived.arrival.accumulated_dwell.run_count",
      "asserted_weight": 0.0
    }
  ]
}
